      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's solve two-number puzzles where the clues involve products, squares or reciprocals.\n\nThese can look harder than ordinary sum-and-difference questions.\n\nA few identities from the chapter turn them into short calculations.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "concept_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "concept_1": {
      "id": "concept_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When you know the sum and product of two numbers, you can find their difference without finding each number first.\n\nUse:\n(x−y)²=(x+y)²−4xy.\n\nThen take the positive square root for the absolute difference.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
